      "title": "Practical quantum advantage depends on benchmarks, access models, and classical baselines",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Why asymptotic speedups are not enough",
          "content": "Quantum machine learning claims identify speedups for pattern recognition, linear algebra, principal components, and linear-system solving, but the same source stresses that input, output, costing, and benchmarking constraints determine whether those speedups become practical. This aligns with NISQ cautions that mature classical approximation methods remain strong competitors and that quantum computers are not expected to efficiently solve hard NP-hard instances in general."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Task-specific demonstrations versus reusable advantage",
          "content": "Random-circuit sampling on Sycamore provides a concrete task-specific super-classical benchmark, while the QML source frames speedup by lower query or gate complexity under particular access assumptions. Together they support a topic centered on distinguishing demonstrable sampling advantage from broadly useful algorithmic advantage."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Evidence boundaries",
          "content": "The available evidence supports a cautious synthesis: advantage is credible for selected tasks and models, but each claimed gain depends on hardware errors, data loading, readout, circuit costs, and comparison against optimized classical methods."
        }
      ],
