      "title": "Short-coherence hybrid algorithms adapt quantum computation to noisy devices",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Hybrid loops reduce coherent runtime demands",
          "content": "The photonic variational eigensolver uses repeated expectation-value measurements inside a classical minimization loop, replacing long coherent phase-estimation evolutions with many short measurements. Quantum machine learning has a parallel theme: quantum subroutines can accelerate selected computations, but circuit depth and implementation overhead can erase nominal exponential gains if realistic costs are not controlled."
        },
        {
          "heading": "NISQ fit and limitations",
          "content": "NISQ evidence suggests devices with tens to hundreds of noisy qubits may be valuable experimental tools, but gate errors limit uncorrected circuit depth. Hybrid methods such as VQE and QML routines are therefore synthesis-worthy as approaches that seek useful results before fault tolerance, while remaining vulnerable to noise, optimization difficulty, and classical competition."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Hardware-aware ansatz design",
          "content": "Device ansatz strategies in the photonic VQE source show one route to adapting algorithms to available hardware. This complements the QML warning that unoptimized realizations, such as large HHL-style circuits, can become prohibitive despite attractive theoretical scaling."
        }
