      "title": "Quantum data workflows seek useful summaries without full state reconstruction",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Avoiding full tomography",
          "content": "Quantum machine learning for quantum data can extract principal components, dynamics, or expectation-value summaries with fewer measurements than full state reconstruction. The quantum-internet source independently warns that full density-matrix characterization fails as networks grow because the state description scales exponentially."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Expectation values as compact observables",
          "content": "The photonic VQE source shows that broad Hamiltonian classes can be reduced to measured Pauli-product expectation values, providing another example of replacing full state knowledge with task-relevant summaries. This connects quantum-data learning, molecular energy estimation, and network verification around the common problem of extracting useful evidence from exponentially large state spaces."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Verification uncertainty",
          "content": "The sources do not establish a universal replacement for tomography. They instead support a family of summary-based strategies whose reliability depends on the target observable, access model, and verification protocol."
        }
      ],
