      "topic_id": "topic-data-access-readout-and-error-overheads",
      "title": "Data access, readout, and error overheads shape whether quantum algorithms scale",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Input and output bottlenecks",
          "content": "The QML source states that loading large classical datasets can require exponential time without qRAM and that reading full solution vectors or principal components can be exponentially hard. These caveats constrain linear-algebra speedups such as HHL and qPCA, especially when the desired answer is classical data rather than a quantum state or compact statistic."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Hardware noise and fault-tolerance costs",
          "content": "NISQ evidence adds that uncorrected noisy circuits cannot grow far before noise overwhelms the signal, while scalable fault-tolerant computing requires large physical-qubit overhead. Sycamore and VQE sources show useful experiments under these constraints, but also expose fidelity, calibration, and measurement limits."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Synthesis claim",
          "content": "Across sources, scalability is not determined by asymptotic gate counts alone. Data loading, measurement demands, circuit depth, error rates, and correction overhead jointly decide whether a proposed quantum advantage remains meaningful."
        }
