      "title": "Special-purpose quantum platforms broaden the near-term learning landscape",
      "structure_type": "wiki",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Beyond universal fault-tolerant machines",
          "content": "The QML source allows deep quantum learning to use quantum annealers, programmable photonic circuits, and quantum Boltzmann machines rather than requiring a large universal quantum computer. This connects to the photonic VQE experiment, which demonstrates a task-specific integrated photonic processor, and to NISQ framing that near-term devices are best understood as experimental tools and stepping stones."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Sampling and thermalization as shared primitives",
          "content": "Quantum Boltzmann machine training claims quadratic improvements in thermalization, sampling, and data access under quantum-coherent or qRAM assumptions. Random-circuit sampling and photonic expectation estimation are different tasks, but they share a broader theme: near-term platforms may be evaluated by how well they produce, sample, or measure quantum distributions under constrained coherence."
        },
        {
          "heading": "Open comparison problem",
          "content": "The evidence supports special-purpose platforms as plausible testbeds, not settled replacements for universal fault-tolerant computation. Their value depends on benchmarked performance against classical methods and on whether hardware-specific advantages survive realistic noise and access assumptions."
        }
